## build
run_chacha_ref.sh compiles four times, all with -O3 -march=native and
-I chacha-ref, renaming the eSTREAM API per round count via -D:
  cc -O3 -march=native -I chacha-ref -c chacha-ref/chacha8.c  -o /tmp/chacha-8.o  \
     -DECRYPT_init=ref8_init  -DECRYPT_keysetup=ref8_keysetup \
     -DECRYPT_ivsetup=ref8_ivsetup -DECRYPT_encrypt_bytes=ref8_encrypt_bytes \
     -DECRYPT_decrypt_bytes=ref8_decrypt_bytes -DECRYPT_keystream_bytes=ref8_keystream_bytes
  (same for chacha12.c -> ref12_*, chacha20.c -> ref20_*)
  cc -O3 -march=native -I chacha-ref -o ./chacha_ref_bench chacha_ref_bench.c \
     /tmp/chacha-8.o /tmp/chacha-12.o /tmp/chacha-20.o
     then rm -f /tmp/chacha-{8,12,20}.o
The script runs under `set -e` and emits no compiler chatter, so the build's
output was empty: zero diagnostics, no warnings, exit status 0 for all four
invocations.  The evidence that the -march=native build actually took is that
the binary ran and printed both self-tests PASS plus RESULT lines -- the
self-tests run the compiled chacha-ref code on TC1 and the 256-bit vector, so a
miscompiled or silently-fallback build would have failed there.

