## reading the numbers
The reference implementation is scalar C written for portability, not speed:
salsa20_wordtobyte() rebuilds a u32 x[16] from the context on every call, runs
the 8/12/20 rounds with a QUARTERROUND macro over that array, and writes the
result with 16 U32TO8_LITTLE byte stores into a u8 output[64]; the caller then
XORs m into c one byte at a time.  So each 64-byte block pays two byte-granular
loops plus an array round-trip that the compiler cannot keep in registers
across the loop.  No SIMD, no multi-block interleave, no counter batch.

On this core that lands at 9.29 c/B for ChaCha20, 5.84 for ChaCha12, 4.34 for
ChaCha8 -- the round-count scaling is close to linear (9.29/5.84 = 1.59 vs
20/12 = 1.67; 5.84/4.34 = 1.35 vs 12/8 = 1.50), as expected once the fixed
per-block overhead is amortised over 64 bytes and the quarter-rounds dominate.

Against the hand-written scalar ChaCha in crypto.c measured on the same host in
crypto-x86-74.log (ChaCha20 697 MB/s = 2.58 c/B, ChaCha12 1038 MB/s = 1.73 c/B,
ChaCha8 1373 MB/s = 1.31 c/B), the Bernstein reference is 3.3-3.6x slower
(2.58/9.30 = 0.28, 1.73/5.84 = 0.30, 1.31/4.34 = 0.30).  That gap is not a
compiler problem: the reference forces the 16-word schedule through memory on
every block and XORs byte-wise, whereas crypto.c keeps the state in named
locals across the whole buffer and vectorises the XOR.

The AES anchor (308.6 MB/s at 8192-byte blocks = 5.81 c/B at 1792 MHz) is only
1.60x cheaper per byte than reference ChaCha20 and 2.2x cheaper than the
hand-written ChaCha20 in crypto.c.  [INFERENCE] 5.8 c/B is far slower than a
Piledriver AES-NI block (roughly 1 c/B for CBC encrypt), so this openssl 3.6.4
build on this host is most likely not reaching the AES-NI path for
aes-128-cbc -- the /proc/cpuinfo `aes` flag is present, so the gap is in the
library/provider or the CBC block-size overhead, not in the CPU.  Treat the
number as a machine anchor only; do not read it as an AES-NI throughput
ceiling.  The stable, comparable facts in this log are the three chacha-ref
RESULT rows, which agree within 0.9% across runs.
